<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  QBX-213. A NOTE PLAYED TO THE COUNT-IN CLICK IS A NOTE PLAYED AFTER THE
  RECORD BUTTON, and the take keeps it.

  The performer presses record, hears N bars of click, and comes in on the
  downbeat - a few tens of milliseconds early, because that is what playing to
  a click is. Before this the first note was simply not in the take, and it
  was lost TWICE OVER, which is why the symptom looked intermittent:

    - on the FIRST take of a session there was no recorder sink at all while
      the click ran (`SMidiInputHub::recorderSink()` is minted on first ask,
      and the first ask was `SMidiRecorder::start()`, which a preamble defers
      to the far end of the click), so the fan-out had nowhere to put the
      message and NOTHING logged the loss;
    - on every later take the sink existed, filled during the click, and
      `start()`'s drain threw the messages away at TW_LOGD.

  THE FIX IS WHERE THE DRAIN BELONGS, not what it does. The record gesture
  begins at `SApplication::startRecording()`, so the recorder acquires its
  sinks and drains them THERE - whatever is in the ring at the instant the
  button goes down really is from before it - and what arrives during the
  preamble is kept. That is the retrospective-capture buffer the hub was
  already keeping the ring alive for.

  THE CATCH RANGE bounds it: `midi/recordCatchRangeMs`, 0..1000, default 100,
  which is Cubase's own number for the same preference. A caught note is
  CLAMPED to the take's start and keeps its end, so it is SHORTENED, never
  moved - the ticket's own words.

  A NOTE IS CAUGHT ON TWO CONDITIONS, and section 1 and section 2 below gate
  one each, in isolation:

    - it began no earlier than the range, because beyond that it was a note
      played during the click rather than at the top of the take, and mashing
      it onto beat 1 is worse than dropping it;
    - it is still SOUNDING when the take begins, because a note that began and
      ended during the click was never an anticipation of the downbeat - both
      its ends would clamp to the same frame and store a note of zero length
      that the performer never played.

  THIS CASE OWNS `midi/recordCatchRangeMs` and writes `transport/countInBars`
  and `transport/preRollBars`, joining the count-in / pre-roll group that owns
  those two between them. All three are put back at the end.

  Env, on the CTest entry: RUN_SERIAL, SMARAGD_CAPTURE_SPEED=1. No audio input
  backend - a MIDI-armed instrument track has no audio input at all, the same
  reading the live_instrument cases take.

  WHY THE TRACK CARRIES AN INSTRUMENT, and it is load-bearing rather than
  decoration. Arming opens the input port only through a LIVE LANE, and an
  armed MIDI track with nothing to play makes no live lane - while
  `CaptureMidiInput::inject()` is a NO-OP on a CLOSED port, so an injection
  into one vanishes without a word. SECTION 4 is what needs it: that section
  plays a key BEFORE the record button, when nothing else has opened the
  port. Sections 1 to 3 would survive without it, because `beginPreCapture()`
  itself opens the port at the button - but an injection that silently goes
  nowhere makes a case pass while measuring nothing, and the first draft of
  the probe behind this ticket did exactly that.

  THE CLOSED FORM. 120 BPM, 4/4, 48 kHz: bar = 96000 frames = 2 s, so a
  TWO-bar count-in is 4000 ms of click. The locator is 96000 (bar 3), not 0,
  so nothing here can be satisfied by a clamp at the start of the project.

  WALL-CLOCK SENSITIVITY, stated rather than left to be discovered. Sections 1
  and 3 place a message 600 ms before the end of the click, so they need the
  count-in not to OVERRUN by more than 400 ms; it ends on delivered frames,
  which can be late under load but never early. Section 2 injects 3000 ms
  before the downbeat against a 100 ms range and section 4 needs no count-in
  at all, so those two have margin in both directions and are the ones to
  trust if they ever disagree with 1 and 3.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="midi_count_in_catch">

  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <add-track index="-1"/>
    <set-tempo bpm="120"/>

    <insert-plugin trackPath="0" slotIndex="0"
                   format="clap" uid="tw.test.clap.sine"
                   name="Smaragd Test Sine" vendor="Smaragd"
                   path="twtestclap.clap" nIn="0" nOut="2"
                   isInstrument="true"/>
    <assert-instrument-slot trackPath="0" present="1" format="clap"
                            uid="tw.test.clap.sine"/>

    <set-track-input trackPath="0" input="midi:capture:any"/>
    <set-count-in bars="2"/>
    <set-pre-roll bars="0"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="1"/>

    <!-- The live lane is what opens the port, and it is on because the track
         is armed with an instrument and the transport is stopped (monitor
         Auto, design D9). This assertion is the one that would fail first if
         the injections below ever started vanishing. -->
    <assert-input-meter trackPath="0" contains="live=on"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         1. THE CAUGHT NOTE - and this is the FIRST take of the session, so
            it is the half that used to fail for want of a sink rather than
            for the drain. Both halves are one fix, but only this ordering
            gates the first of them.
         ================================================================= -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordCatchRangeMs" value="1000"/>

    <record-start/>
    <!-- 3400 ms into a 4000 ms count-in: key 60 goes down ~600 ms EARLY,
         inside the 1000 ms range, and is HELD past the downbeat - which is
         what anticipating a downbeat is. It is released at 4400 ms, so about
         400 ms of it belongs inside the take. -->
    <wait-ms ms="3400"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>

    <!-- Key 62 goes down 100 ms later, INSIDE the same range, and is released
         BEFORE the downbeat. It was a note played during the click, not an
         anticipation of anything, and admitting it would store a note of zero
         length at the take's start. It must be dropped - and dropped for the
         sustain rule, not for the range, which is why it sits inside the
         range on purpose. -->
    <wait-ms ms="100"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="62" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="62" channel="0"/>

    <!-- ...and key 60 is let go 400 ms after the take began. -->
    <wait-ms ms="700"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="60" channel="0"/>

    <!-- key 64 is played well inside the take, as the control. -->
    <wait-ms ms="600"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="64" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="64" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- TWO notes: key 60, caught, and key 64, ordinary. `clamped=1` is key
         60 being pulled to the take's start and `uncaught=1` is key 62 being
         refused - and asserting BOTH counters is what tells the two rules
         apart. Neither number is wall-clock dependent at the margins this
         case leaves. -->
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="1" passes="1" notes="2"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" contains="clamped=1"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" contains="uncaught=1"/>

    <!-- WHERE key 60 LANDED: at the take's own zero, exactly. No tolerance -
         a clamp is an assignment, not a measurement. -->
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" count="2"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="60"
                        at="0" tolerance="0" count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="62"
                        count="0"/>

    <!-- ...and it was SHORTENED, not moved: released 400 ms after the take
         began, which is 19200 frames at 48 kHz, and that is where the release
         still is. This assertion is the whole ticket in one line - a note
         that is merely moved would end 200 ms later than it starts, and a
         note that is dropped would not be here at all. -->
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteoff" key="60"
                        at="19200" tolerance="4800" count="1"/>

    <undo/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="0"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         2. THE UNCAUGHT NOTE, and the RANGE is what refuses it. The note is
            held past the downbeat, so the sustain rule of section 1 would
            admit it - it is 3000 ms early against a 100 ms range and that
            alone is why it is dropped. Isolating the two rules is the point
            of the two sections.
         ================================================================= -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordCatchRangeMs" value="100"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>

    <record-start/>
    <!-- 1000 ms into the click: a performer noodling, not coming in. -->
    <wait-ms ms="1000"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="60" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <!-- ...still holding it 400 ms after the take began -->
    <wait-ms ms="3400"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="60" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="600"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="64" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="64" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <!-- ONE note, and the survivor is key 64. `uncaught=1` is the range doing
         the refusing, which is what tells this apart from the DEFECT: before
         the fix the count-in note was missing with `uncaught=0` and `pre=0`,
         because nothing had captured it at all. A case that only counted
         notes could not see that difference. -->
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="1" passes="1" notes="1"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" contains="uncaught=1"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" contains="clamped=0"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="64"
                        count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="60"
                        count="0"/>

    <undo/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="0"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         3. A CANCELLED PREAMBLE KEEPS NOTHING, and the KEY IS STILL DOWN when
            it is cancelled - which is the only shape that gates this.

            The first draft of this section injected a short note early in the
            click and then checked that it was absent from the next take. It
            passed with the cancel REMOVED, because that note was ~1.3 s
            before the next take and the catch range refused it on its own:
            the section was gating section 2's rule a second time, under
            another name. So the note here is held ACROSS the cancel and
            released inside the next take - inside the range and still
            sounding at its start, so the range would admit it and only
            dropping the buffer keeps it out.
         ================================================================= -->
    <set-option key="midi/recordCatchRangeMs" value="1000"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>

    <record-start/>
    <!-- 3400 ms into the 4000 ms click, and the finger STAYS DOWN -->
    <wait-ms ms="3400"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="67" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <!-- STILL INSIDE THE COUNT-IN, so this CANCELS rather than stops: nothing
         has been recorded, so there is nothing to commit and nothing to undo.
         Until QBX-213 this verb refused outright - it asked
         `isRecordingActive()`, which is false while the click runs - so the
         cancel path in `SApplication::stopRecording()` had never been
         reachable from a script at all. -->
    <record-stop/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="0"/>

    <!-- A real take, with the count-in off so this claim is about the
         CANCELLED buffer and not about a second preamble. Key 67 is released
         400 ms in - a note-off that pairs with nothing, because the note-on it
         belonged to went with the cancelled preamble. -->
    <set-count-in bars="0"/>
    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="400"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="67" channel="0"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="72" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="72" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="1" notes="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="72"
                        count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="67"
                        count="0"/>

    <undo/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="0"/>

    <!-- =================================================================
         4. WITH NO PREAMBLE, A KEY PRESSED BEFORE THE BUTTON IS STILL NOT IN
            THE TAKE. This is the drain's original purpose, and QBX-213 did
            not widen it: the count-in case is different only because there
            the button is four seconds EARLIER than the recorder's start.
            Without this section the fix could have become "keep everything",
            and nothing would have noticed.
         ================================================================= -->
    <set-locator position="96000"/>
    <!-- armed, stopped, monitoring: the port is open and the fan-out is
         filling the recorder's ring right now, with no take in sight. -->
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="55" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="55" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>

    <record-start/>
    <wait-ms ms="400"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteon" key="76" velocity="100" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="200"/>
    <midi-in-event kind="noteoff" key="76" channel="0"/>
    <wait-ms ms="300"/>
    <record-stop/>

    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" clips="1" notes="1"/>
    <assert-midi-recorded trackPath="0" contains="pre=0"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="76"
                        count="1"/>
    <assert-midi-events scope="clip" clip="0,0" kind="noteon" key="55"
                        count="0"/>

    <arm-track trackPath="0" armed="0"/>

    <!-- The three keys this case wrote, put back. -->
    <set-count-in bars="0"/>
    <set-pre-roll bars="0"/>
    <set-option key="midi/recordCatchRangeMs" value="100"/>
  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <assert-track-count equals="1"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
